  create a great video  the meritocracy of the internet will  take over and you can have incredibly  outsized returns by using  this kind of productized leverage that's  out there so  um those are two on the well side on the  happiness side you know there's a lot of  kind of  little habits in there and and things  that he recommends and  um pieces of philosophy that he pulls in  but really that all starts with  realizing that  we're going to be here a very short time  in the scheme of things like  life is short the universe has been here  a long time it'll be here a long time  um and realizing that any moment that  you are not  choosing to be happy and choosing to  interpret the inputs of the world in a  positive way  you're not doing anyone any favors like  you're not making you're not making  yourself better  off you're not making the people around  you better off  and you are in control of that like you  can choose to be happy you can choose  positive interpretations  and when you do everyone around you and  you are better off for it  but no one can choose that for you you  have to choose it for yourself  um and so it is a you have to kind of  zoom all the way out to get back to this  tiny little  choice and this tiny little thing that  we all have but it's kind of a  core core piece and just reminding  yourself of that as often as you  possibly can can be a really  transformative thing for your for your  mindset and your mood and  and everything just kind of comes easier  when you remember that like it doesn't  have to be hard it doesn't have to be  miserable  you're not you're not more productive  because you're unhappy like  just choose a positive take on all of  this and like keep going  were there any surprises for you in  terms of uh putting this together um  either from the content or the process  yeah there were definitely some i mean  there were a lot of unknowns when i  started this project right  it came up so fast um kind of out of  nowhere  that at first my first idea was like hey  i put together like a little tiny ebook  you know a little thing those you know  20 pages  and then when i had all this material in  front of me i was like oh my god this is  this is huge i'm going to make a you  know 600 page version of it i'm going to  cover every category this is going to be  port charlie's almanac  maybe i should just build like a tool  that actually allows for live updating  and it's more of kind of like  a blog's thing that does it would be  have to be like a little transformed  because it's not about whatever's newest  it's about the best ideas and how you  combine them all  so there was a lot of kind of creative  crossroads along the way i wasn't sure  if i should be writing about naval's  ideas or  or just kind of getting out of the way  and letting them speak for themselves  um so it was tough to kind of navigate  some of those things and  find the right mix of constraints that  let me keep making progress  um i'm really really happy with where we  ended up there's every chance that that  this could have become something totally  different um if someone had given me  kind of a different  push along the way um trevor mckendree  actually writes like an awesome  newsletter  um i like brought him a mess of a  manuscript early on and i was like i  don't even know what this thing is  supposed to be  like is it going to be a blog is it  going to be a book  like how are we going to get there from  here and he's like don't ever think it's  a book just  get back to work um keep making it a  book  i was like okay that actually like  clears up a lot it just took the right  person to kind of like  slap me and and get me back on the road  and now all of a sudden i'm making  progress again  um the other the other kind of big thing  was just like how comprehensive should  it be  you know there was my first draft of  this manuscript was  600 pages it was well over 100 000 words  it was huge  um the final product for reference is  like less than 50 000  which is like much more of a normal book  but this was an encyclopedia for a  minute of like  everything interesting that i thought  naval had ever said  um which included you know sections on  blockchain and education  and investing and building startups  because all like  this is such a fun project for me  because i'm interested in all of the  stuff that nimal has said  um i think there's there's thought  experiments  and you know futurism and there's so  much stuff in there that i thought was  was important and interesting and  helpful um and it took a really kind of  broad wide range of peer reviewers kind  of  after that manuscript who i gave them a  600 page thing and they're like you'll  be [ __ ] kidding me  um and they all read like a different 60  percent  um but the all all of them loved wealth  and happiness  and so that was kind of a go figure  right like everybody wants to be  wealthy and happy and so those became  like the foundation of it and figuring  out what to put around that to give it  the right context  make it like a fast engaging kind of  awesome read i really want people to  power through the end of the book and be  like oh my god i wish this was longer  like that's the best compliment a book  can receive  um and all those sections are now kind  of published  on on the ballmenack.com so there's no  reason not to have them on the website  and put them out there for people who  are interested in it  but i really wanted them to power  through the book and then find their way  to kind of  different rabbit holes um based on what  they were interested in  but that that early kind of feedback on  the first first manuscript was  so key to get me through some of the  some of the things that  um was hard to see past what i was  interested in and and learn kind of what  other people  would want to see in it yeah it feels  like you've had this um  very intimate experience where you're  like spending all day with  uh the thoughts of somebody uh and  obviously it being the ball is um  is cool and valuable but how do you  think you've changed  just from spending that much time kind  of re-reading  so much of this kind of high quality  content uh and i've got to imagine  almost like searing it into your brain  to the point where like  you can literally call it back like a  database right of just like hey i know  exactly what  the thoughts are on this topic or that  topic like how have you changed  or maybe just your perspective changed  yeah it's it's actually  um i think you know when you've changed  when it's hard to pinpoint how you've  changed  you know um i definitely i mean i read  this book and  some of the source material like more  than 20 times in the course of building  this thing over  three years like there's hundreds and  hundreds of hours into this  um of just sitting with this material  and picking up a piece and trying to  kind of totally  figure out totally understand it and  figure out what else it relates to  um so i almost feel guilty at like how  many things i'm sure i adopted as my own  in this process that i'm like  this is now my idea but like it  definitely came from from this process  in this book  um but i think you know we are we are  all doing that we are all standing on  many layers of  shoulders of giants um so it has been  kind of part of that process but the the  this  kind of crucible of editing that i've  been going through  the reward for that is that i now have  like a little  like naval who sits on my shoulder like  he is on my mental board of directors i  have like a hogwarts portrait in my head  that to such an extent that like i can  almost ask it questions  and know what it it would say back um  and that that is a really high bar you  know you you don't have time to do that  with very many people  in in your life um to study an idea that  deeply or a person that deeply  and i'm very kind of grateful that  you know it was a very kind of a  luck-based thing that like this became  the book that i was working on like  i tweet dumb stuff all the time this one  just happened to turn into a book  and i'm very lucky that like this agreed  with  so much of what i wanted to know and  what i wanted to learn like um  um it would have been hard to sit with  three years of just anybody so like  picking who to study and who to kind of  whose ideas to let this deeply into your  head i think is is important and i'm  really glad that  um that we're over that threshold and  that like this is the bar that i reached  in working on this book  and you know for me personally it was it  was transformative to a point where like  i don't i would love everyone in the  world to read it and get something out  of it but i don't need that to feel good  about having spent this time  working on it like this was this is a  project for me from the beginning  um and i hope everyone gets something  out of it and i think many people  love it but i'm also would not be  disappointed in the time invested if all  it did was change me  and teach me things

